{"links":[{"id":"165b47d3fd33","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.de_sacerdotio","w":"chrysostomus.de_sacerdotio","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":4,"p":[10,10],"r":[3,15],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet iii. 15; \" Haud seio an ita loqui possit primatus romani defensor. \" Bengel's Edition of this Treatise, Leipzig, 1834.p. 145, note 17."},{"id":"53eafcb92371","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_mt","w":"chrysostomus.hom_mt","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":2,"p":[6,6],"r":[3,19],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"see 1 Pet. iii. 19, 20; iv. 6 ; St. Iren. iv. 45; iii. 23; iv. 39, 56, 66; v. 31 ; Orig. in Joan, t. ii. 30; contr. Cels. ii. 43; in Rom. lib. 5, l. t. iv. 551. B; Tert. de Anim. 7; St. Greg. Naz. Or. 42 p 693. Ed. Morell; and others cited by Cotelerius on St. Hermas. iii. 16."},{"id":"1e780711d5c5","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_mt","w":"chrysostomus.hom_mt","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":7,"p":[39,39],"r":[3,7],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Cor. vii. 3. In our copies of the Greek Testament, and in the Mss. of St. Chrysostom, here it is, eu!noian , not τιμὴν . But Mr. Field writes τιμὴν , 1. from internal evidence ; 2, from comparison of St. Chrysostom's own Commentary on this place of St. Paul; and accounts for it by supposing that he quoted from memory, as often, and confused the verse with, 1 Peter iii. 7. [The text in 1 Cor. vii. 3, according to most of the best Greek and Latin Mss., is τὴν ὀφειλήν R. V., \"her duo\"). The text and argument of Chrysostom indicate careless citation. The translators note was written before New Testament textual criticism had received any attention from more modern English divines.-R.]"},{"id":"2a82b94fa5c2","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_mt","w":"chrysostomus.hom_mt","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":33,"p":[38,38],"r":[3,15],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1Peter iii. 15."},{"id":"f5cc1e9bb71e","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_mt","w":"chrysostomus.hom_mt","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":85,"p":[9,9],"r":[5,13],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1Pet. v. 13."},{"id":"522d37c9df43","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_jo","w":"chrysostomus.hom_jo","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":27,"p":[10,10],"r":[2,22],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet. ii. 22"},{"id":"20e0d1365e28","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_jo","w":"chrysostomus.hom_jo","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":42,"p":[28,28],"r":[1,24],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet. i. 24"},{"id":"b6bca126df50","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_jo","w":"chrysostomus.hom_jo","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":59,"p":[36,36],"r":[5,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet. v. 8"},{"id":"2780777351cb","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_jo","w":"chrysostomus.hom_jo","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":82,"p":[5,5],"r":[2,22],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet. ii. 22"},{"id":"95060a8429df","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":25,"p":[1,1],"r":[4,16],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"The name Christians was probably given by the Gentiles. The word appears but twice, besides here, in the N. T. (Acts xxvi. 8; 1 Pet. iv. 16), and in both cases it is implied that the name was a name applied to the disciples of Jesus by others. The Jews could hardly have originated the name since Christ was to them but the Greek equivalent for their sacred name Messiah, and from that word they would not have formed a name for the hated sect. The Jews called them rather Nazarenes (Acts xxiv. 5). The Romans seem to have misunderstood the origin of the name, as Tacitus says: A uctor nominis ejus (Christiani) Christus, as if Christus was an appellative instead of a title.-G. B. S."},{"id":"e359081774c8","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":26,"p":[2,2],"r":[5,7],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet. v. 7"},{"id":"506d46e5c561","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":32,"p":[5,5],"r":[5,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet. v. 8"},{"id":"8143c58a26fd","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":40,"p":[3,3],"r":[4,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet. iv. 8"},{"id":"faf07ccdb07a","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":54,"p":[3,3],"r":[5,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hom. xxiii. in Gen. §, 6, p. 215, A. \"I exhort you never to lay the blame upon Satan, but upon your own remissness. I say not this to exculpate him, for he `goeth about, 0' etc. 1 Pet. v. 8, but to put ourselves in more security, that we may not exculpate ourselves when we so easily go over to the evil one, that we may not speak those heartless, senseless words, `Why has God left the evil one so much freedom to seduce men. 0' These words betoken the greatest ingratitude. Consider this: God has left him that freedom, to this very end, that by fear of the enemy he may keep us ever watchful and sober.\""},{"id":"ba0d54a0fab0","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_rom","w":"chrysostomus.hom_rom","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":16,"p":[17,17],"r":[3,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Pet. iii. 21"},{"id":"966ba2944263","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_1co","w":"chrysostomus.hom_1co","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":3,"p":[27,27],"r":[3,15],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Peter chapter 3, verse 15"},{"id":"63322e173165","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_1co","w":"chrysostomus.hom_1co","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":26,"p":[54,54],"r":[3,6],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Peter chapter 3, verse 6"},{"id":"2c39f961e57c","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_2co","w":